./logs/iter_001_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:932:The sparse vanishing-spike refutation checks out under the literal per-process reading of the conjecture. It gives a process with \(\beta(h)=1\) for every \(h\), yet the learner that always posts \(0\) has regret at most \(1\) for every horizon. So the stated “only if” direction is false as written.
./logs/iter_001_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:934:The core flaw exposed is that \(\beta\) is an unweighted delay-tail condition: it counts long delays for quotes whose payoff scale is negligible, especially \(a=0\).
./logs/iter_001_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:949:- Hence for every \(h\), choosing \(n\) with \(2^n>h\) gives \(\beta(h)=1\).
./logs/iter_001_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:959:**Lemma 1, audited valid.** The sparse-spike process has \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\).
./logs/iter_001_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:973:with \(0<c_n\le C\). Then \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\), but the always-zero learner has regret at most \(C\). Thus the counterexample can be scaled to have arbitrarily small constant regret. In particular, it is below any diverging sublinear regret envelope eventually.
./logs/iter_001_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:977:The \(\beta\)-calculation is sound because the definition takes \(\sup_a\), and \(a=0\) is allowed. Conditioning on \(P_1,\dots,P_{s_n}\) does not reveal \(U_{n+1}\), but \(U_{n+1}>0\) almost surely, so the next positive price occurs exactly at \(s_{n+1}\).
./logs/iter_001_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:989:The “if” direction still depends on formalizing the reduction to full-information delayed expert learning, but that is separate from this refutation.
./logs/iter_001_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:993:The sparse vanishing-spike process is a decisive obstruction to the stated iff. Long finite hitting times can occur with probability one for actions whose reward is zero or arbitrarily small, so unweighted tail decay is too strong as a necessary condition.
