./iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:3:The sparse vanishing-spike refutation checks out under the literal per-process reading of the conjecture. It gives a process with \(\beta(h)=1\) for every \(h\), yet the learner that always posts \(0\) has regret at most \(1\) for every horizon. So the stated “only if” direction is false as written.
./iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:5:The core flaw exposed is that \(\beta\) is an unweighted delay-tail condition: it counts long delays for quotes whose payoff scale is negligible, especially \(a=0\).
./iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:20:- Hence for every \(h\), choosing \(n\) with \(2^n>h\) gives \(\beta(h)=1\).
./iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:30:**Lemma 1, audited valid.** The sparse-spike process has \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\).
./iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:44:with \(0<c_n\le C\). Then \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\), but the always-zero learner has regret at most \(C\). Thus the counterexample can be scaled to have arbitrarily small constant regret. In particular, it is below any diverging sublinear regret envelope eventually.
./iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:48:The \(\beta\)-calculation is sound because the definition takes \(\sup_a\), and \(a=0\) is allowed. Conditioning on \(P_1,\dots,P_{s_n}\) does not reveal \(U_{n+1}\), but \(U_{n+1}>0\) almost surely, so the next positive price occurs exactly at \(s_{n+1}\).
./iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:60:The “if” direction still depends on formalizing the reduction to full-information delayed expert learning, but that is separate from this refutation.
