./logs/iter_001_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:769:with \(0<c_n\le C\). Then \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\), but the always-zero learner has regret at most \(C\). Thus the counterexample can be scaled to have arbitrarily small constant regret. In particular, it is below any diverging sublinear regret envelope eventually.
./logs/iter_001_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:773:The \(\beta\)-calculation is sound because the definition takes \(\sup_a\), and \(a=0\) is allowed. Conditioning on \(P_1,\dots,P_{s_n}\) does not reveal \(U_{n+1}\), but \(U_{n+1}>0\) almost surely, so the next positive price occurs exactly at \(s_{n+1}\).
./logs/iter_001_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:785:The “if” direction still depends on formalizing the reduction to full-information delayed expert learning, but that is separate from this refutation.
